@@ -0,0 +1,640 @@
+# Codex Task: Simple Code Runner in Go
+
+## Goal
+
+Create a simple educational code runner in Go.
+
+The runner should execute user code inside Docker containers using:
+
+```go
+github.com/moby/moby/client
+```
+
+Supported runtimes:
+
+- GCC
+- Clang
+- Python
+
+This is an educational project.
+
+Keep the implementation simple.
+
+Do not add unnecessary abstractions, queues, Kubernetes, worker pools, complicated sandboxing, databases, or distributed systems.
+
+---
+
+# Expected usage
+
+The runner receives:
+
+```go
+type RunRequest struct {
+    Runtime string
+    Code    string
+    Input   string
+}
+```
+
+Supported runtime values:
+
+```text
+gcc
+clang
+python
+```
+
+It returns:
+
+```go
+type RunResult struct {
+    Stdout   string
+    Stderr   string
+    ExitCode int
+}
+```
+
+Main method:
+
+```go
+func (r *Runner) Run(
+    ctx context.Context,
+    req RunRequest,
+) (RunResult, error)
+```
+
+---
+
+# Project structure
+
+Keep the structure small:
+
+```text
+internal/
+└── runner/
+    ├── runner.go
+    ├── docker.go
+    └── types.go
+```
+
+Do not create separate packages for every language.
+
+---
+
+# Runner
+
+Create:
+
+```go
+type Runner struct {
+    docker *client.Client
+}
+```
+
+Constructor:
+
+```go
+func New() (*Runner, error) {
+    cli, err := client.New(
+        client.FromEnv,
+    )
+    if err != nil {
+        return nil, err
+    }
+
+    return &Runner{
+        docker: cli,
+    }, nil
+}
+```
+
+Reuse the Docker client.
+
+---
+
+# Runtime configuration
+
+Use one simple map.
+
+Example:
+
+```go
+type runtimeConfig struct {
+    Image    string
+    FileName string
+    Command  []string
+}
+```
+
+Example configuration:
+
+```go
+var runtimes = map[string]runtimeConfig{
+    "python": {
+        Image:    "python:3.13-alpine",
+        FileName: "main.py",
+        Command: []string{
+            "python3",
+            "/code/main.py",
+        },
+    },
+
+    "gcc": {
+        Image:    "gcc:15",
+        FileName: "main.cpp",
+        Command: []string{
+            "sh",
+            "-c",
+            "g++ /code/main.cpp -std=c++20 -O2 -o /tmp/main && /tmp/main",
+        },
+    },
+
+    "clang": {
+        Image:    "clang-image",
+        FileName: "main.cpp",
+        Command: []string{
+            "sh",
+            "-c",
+            "clang++ /code/main.cpp -std=c++20 -O2 -o /tmp/main && /tmp/main",
+        },
+    },
+}
+```
+
+Use a real pinned Clang image available in the project.
+
+Do not use a separate compiler abstraction.
+
+---
+
+# Execution flow
+
+The whole runner can work like this:
+
+```text
+Run()
+ |
+ +--> find runtime config
+ |
+ +--> create temp directory
+ |
+ +--> write source file
+ |
+ +--> create Docker container
+ |
+ +--> mount temp directory as /code
+ |
+ +--> start container
+ |
+ +--> send stdin
+ |
+ +--> wait
+ |
+ +--> read stdout/stderr
+ |
+ +--> remove container
+ |
+ +--> remove temp directory
+ |
+ +--> return result
+```
+
+Keep this logic easy to read.
+
+---
+
+# Temporary directory
+
+Create:
+
+```go
+dir, err := os.MkdirTemp("", "runner-*")
+```
+
+Cleanup:
+
+```go
+defer os.RemoveAll(dir)
+```
+
+Write source:
+
+```go
+filePath := filepath.Join(
+    dir,
+    config.FileName,
+)
+
+err = os.WriteFile(
+    filePath,
+    []byte(req.Code),
+    0644,
+)
+```
+
+---
+
+# Docker container
+
+Create one container for each call to `Run`.
+
+For Python:
+
+```text
+python3 /code/main.py
+```
+
+For GCC:
+
+```text
+g++ /code/main.cpp -std=c++20 -O2 -o /tmp/main
+/tmp/main
+```
+
+For Clang:
+
+```text
+clang++ /code/main.cpp -std=c++20 -O2 -o /tmp/main
+/tmp/main
+```
+
+Compilation and execution can happen in the same container.
+
+Do not create separate compile and run containers.
+
+---
+
+# Docker mount
+
+Mount the temporary directory into:
+
+```text
+/code
+```
+
+Example concept:
+
+```text
+host:
+/tmp/runner-123
+
+container:
+/code
+```
+
+The source file will therefore be:
+
+```text
+/code/main.cpp
+```
+
+or:
+
+```text
+/code/main.py
+```
+
+---
+
+# stdin
+
+The solution should support stdin.
+
+Example request:
+
+```go
+RunRequest{
+    Runtime: "gcc",
+
+    Code: `
+#include <iostream>
+
+int main() {
+    int a, b;
+    std::cin >> a >> b;
+    std::cout << a + b << '\n';
+}
+`,
+
+    Input: "10 20\n",
+}
+```
+
+Expected stdout:
+
+```text
+30
+```
+
+Use Docker attach to send:
+
+```text
+10 20
+```
+
+to the container stdin.
+
+---
+
+# stdout and stderr
+
+Collect:
+
+```text
+stdout
+stderr
+```
+
+Return them in:
+
+```go
+RunResult
+```
+
+Example:
+
+```go
+RunResult{
+    Stdout:   "30\n",
+    Stderr:   "",
+    ExitCode: 0,
+}
+```
+
+If compilation fails, compiler output can simply be returned through:
+
+```text
+stderr
+```
+
+and:
+
+```text
+ExitCode != 0
+```
+
+Do not create complicated compile-error types yet.
+
+---
+
+# Timeout
+
+Add one simple timeout.
+
+Example:
+
+```go
+runCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(
+    ctx,
+    3*time.Second,
+)
+defer cancel()
+```
+
+If execution exceeds the timeout:
+
+```text
+kill the container
+return an error
+```
+
+This is enough for the educational version.
+
+---
+
+# Basic limits
+
+Use only basic Docker limits.
+
+For example:
+
+```text
+Memory: 256 MB
+CPU: 1
+Network: disabled
+```
+
+Do not implement advanced sandboxing.
+
+The important part is to prevent an obvious infinite loop from running forever.
+
+---
+
+# Cleanup
+
+Always remove the Docker container.
+
+Example:
+
+```go
+defer func() {
+    cleanupCtx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(
+        context.Background(),
+        5*time.Second,
+    )
+    defer cancel()
+
+    // ContainerRemove(...)
+}()
+```
+
+Also remove the temporary directory.
+
+---
+
+# Example Python
+
+Request:
+
+```go
+RunRequest{
+    Runtime: "python",
+
+    Code: `
+a, b = map(int, input().split())
+print(a + b)
+`,
+
+    Input: "10 20\n",
+}
+```
+
+Expected:
+
+```go
+RunResult{
+    Stdout:   "30\n",
+    Stderr:   "",
+    ExitCode: 0,
+}
+```
+
+---
+
+# Example GCC
+
+Code:
+
+```cpp
+#include <iostream>
+
+int main() {
+    int a, b;
+    std::cin >> a >> b;
+    std::cout << a + b << '\n';
+}
+```
+
+Input:
+
+```text
+10 20
+```
+
+Output:
+
+```text
+30
+```
+
+---
+
+# Example Clang
+
+Use the same C++ source.
+
+Only the compiler changes:
+
+```text
+g++
+```
+
+to:
+
+```text
+clang++
+```
+
+---
+
+# Implementation order
+
+Implement in this order:
+
+```text
+1. RunRequest and RunResult
+2. connect to Docker
+3. create temp directory
+4. Python execution
+5. stdin/stdout
+6. GCC execution
+7. Clang execution
+8. timeout
+9. basic memory/CPU limits
+10. cleanup
+```
+
+Do not start with a large architecture.
+
+First make this work:
+
+```text
+Go
+ |
+ v
+Runner.Run()
+ |
+ v
+Docker
+ |
+ +--> Python
+ +--> GCC
+ +--> Clang
+ |
+ v
+stdout + stderr + exit code
+```
+
+---
+
+# Important
+
+This is an educational project.
+
+Prefer:
+
+```text
+simple code
+few files
+clear functions
+easy debugging
+```
+
+over:
+
+```text
+interfaces everywhere
+factories
+registries
+dependency injection frameworks
+worker pools
+distributed execution
+advanced sandbox architecture
+```
+
+A simple `switch` is acceptable.
+
+For example:
+
+```go
+switch req.Runtime {
+case "python":
+    ...
+case "gcc":
+    ...
+case "clang":
+    ...
+default:
+    return RunResult{}, errors.New(
+        "unsupported runtime",
+    )
+}
+```
+
+This is completely fine for this project.
+
+---
+
+# Definition of done
+
+This should work:
+
+```go
+result, err := runner.Run(
+    ctx,
+    RunRequest{
+        Runtime: "python",
+        Code: `
+a, b = map(int, input().split())
+print(a + b)
+`,
+        Input: "10 20\n",
+    },
+)
+```
+
+and return:
+
+```text
+stdout: 30
+exit code: 0
+```
+
+The same runner must also execute:
+
+```text
+GCC C++
+Clang C++
+```
+
+That is enough for the first version.
